## 8. SQL Injection Techniques — Quick Comparison

| Technique | Primary Observable Signal |
|---|---|
| Error-Based SQLi | Database error messages |
| Union-Based SQLi | Query results displayed in the web response |
| Authentication Bypass | Unexpected authentication behavior |
| Boolean-Based Blind SQLi | Differences in true/false application responses |
| Time-Based Blind SQLi | Differences in response timing |
| Out-of-Band SQLi | Separate DNS or HTTP interactions |

---

## 9. Key Takeaways

1. SQL Injection commonly results from unsafe construction of SQL queries using untrusted input.
2. UNION operations require matching column counts and compatible corresponding data types.
3. In-Band SQLi communicates results through the same channel used for the injection.
4. Blind SQLi relies on observable behavior or timing rather than directly displayed query results.
5. Out-of-Band SQLi uses a separate communication channel.
6. Prepared statements and parameterized queries are the primary prevention method.
7. Input validation, least privilege, safe error handling, and WAFs provide additional security layers.
8. SQL Injection findings should be verified and documented using reliable evidence.
9. Security testing must be performed only on systems for which explicit authorization has been granted.

